2026-06-30 15:21:35,698 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-5b45e78d-ba2e-4824-ab21-9f6fe16902b9', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: disease\nsdrf_column: characteristics[disease]\nontology: EFO:0000408, MONDO, DOID, PATO\ndescription: Disease state of the sample.\ninstructions: For healthy/normal samples use "normal" (PATO:0000461). For diseased\n  samples use MONDO or DOID terms. Return null only if completely unstated.\n  Most XL-MS samples are "normal" unless explicitly studying a disease model.\n\nterm: phenotype\nsdrf_column: characteristics[phenotype]\nontology: EFO:0000651, PATO, EFO\ndescription: Observable characteristics or traits (drug sensitivity, molecular markers,\n  expression phenotypes, environmental responses).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. This captures how the sample behaves/appears\n  (distinct from disease diagnosis).\n\nterm: treatment\nsdrf_column: characteristics[treatment]\nontology: EFO:0000727, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Treatment or perturbation applied to the sample (drug, stimulus,\n  environmental stress, growth condition change).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable. Examples: "doxycycline\n  treatment", "heat shock", "DMSO vehicle control".\n\nterm: compound\nsdrf_column: characteristics[compound]\nontology: CHEBI:24431, CHEBI, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Chemical compound or drug applied to the sample.\ninstructions: Use ChEBI if possible. Return null if no compound treatment. Do NOT\n  include the crosslinking reagent here — that goes in comment[cross-linker].\n\nterm: dose\nsdrf_column: characteristics[dose]\nontology: NCIT:C25488\ndescription: Dose or concentration of compound treatment.\npattern: number + unit (e.g. 10 mg/kg, 50 uM, 100 nM, 10 mg/mL)\ninstructions: Return null if no compound treatment or dose not stated.\n\nterm: genetic modification\nsdrf_column: characteristics[genetic modification]\nontology: EFO:0000510, EFO\ndescription: Method of genetic modification (knockout, knockdown, overexpression,\n  transduction). Describes HOW the modification was done.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: perturbation\nsdrf_column: characteristics[perturbation]\nontology: PRIDE:0000700\ndescription: Type of perturbation applied (environmental/experimental perturbation).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: depletion\nsdrf_column: characteristics[depletion]\nontology: PRIDE:0000020\ndescription: Whether abundant protein depletion was performed.\nallowed_values: no depletion, depletion, not applicable\ninstructions: Return "no depletion" if not mentioned. Return "depletion" if\n  high-abundance protein removal is described (e.g. albumin depletion).\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nChemical Probing Provides Insight into the Native Assembly State of a Bacterial Microcompartment\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nSUMMARY\nBacterial microcompartments (BMCs) are widespread in bacteria and are used for a variety of metabolic purposes, including catabolism of host metabolites. A suite of proteins self-assembles into the shell and cargo layers of BMCs. However, the native assembly state of these large complexes remains to be elucidated. Herein, chemical probes were used to observe structural features of a native BMC. While the exterior could be demarcated with fluorophores, the interior was unexpectedly permeable, suggesting the shell layer may be more dynamic than thought. This allowed access to cross-linking chemical probes, which were analyzed to uncover the protein interactome. These cross-links revealed a complex multivalent network among cargo proteins that contained encapsulation peptides and demonstrated that the shell layer follows discreet rules in its assembly. These results are consistent overall with a model where biomolecular condensation drives interactions of cargo proteins prior to envelopment by shell layer proteins.\neTOC Blurb\nBacterial microcompartments (BMC) are large organelle-like structures. They contain enzymes for a particular metabolic pathway surrounded by an icosahedral protein shell. The rules governing their assembly have not been fully revealed. In their manuscript, Trettel et al. observe key structural features of a native BMC using chemical probes and crosslinkers.\nGraphical Abstract\n\n[METHODS]\nSTAR METHODS\nRESOURCE AVAILABILITY\nLead Contact\nFurther information can requests for resources and reagents should be directed to and will be fulfilled by the lead contact, Wade Winkler (wwinkler@umd.edu)\nMaterials availability\nThis study did not generate new unique reagents.\nData and code availability\nMass spectrometry datasets generated by this study have been publicly deposited. The mass spectrometry raw files acquired at the Proteomics Laboratory at NYU (including BS(PEG)5, DSBU cross-linking, and hydroxyl-footprinting data) are accessible at https://massive.ucsd.edu under accession MassIVE MSV000088460. The mass spectrometry proteomics data for DSSO datasets have been deposited to the ProteomeXchange Consortium via the PRIDE partner repository with the dataset identifier PXD029675.\nAny additional information required to reanalyze the data reported in this paper is available from the lead contact upon request.\nEXPERIMENTAL MODEL AND SUBJECT DETAILS\nPduST + R995\nThis E. coli strain was used as a source of Pdu microcompartments. This strain contains kanamycin resistance. Accordingly, PduST + R995 was always grown in 2xYT media supplemented with kanamycin at 37°C. Supplementation with 1,2-propanediol to 0.5% (v/v) was used to induce expression of Pdu microcompartments. Specifics about Pdu BMC purification can be found in Method Details.\nDT074\nThis E. coli strain was used as a source of hexahistidine-tagged PduA S93C shell protein for purification. DT074 uses a pUC-based vector encoding for ampicillin resistance. Accordingly, DT074 was always grown in 2xYT media supplemented with carbenicillin at 37°C. Specifics about PduA purification can be found in Method Details.\nMETHOD DETAILS\nProbe and Cross-Linker Preparation\nCross-linkers and fluorescent probes used in this study were prepared as smaller single-use aliquots to extend their utility and cut down on costs. AF488, AF532 and AF680 were resuspended in 1:9 (v/v) water:acetonitrile and aliquoted into 40 nmol portions in 1.5 mL microcentrifuge tubes. Aliquots were then vacuum evaporated, sealed, and stored at −20°C under desiccant in the dark. The cross-linkers BS(PEG)5 and sulfo-EGS were prepared similarly but resuspended in anhydrous, degassed DMSO in a dehumidified anaerobic hood prior to being aliquoted. Aliquots were then sealed and stored at −20°C under desiccant. DSBU and BS3 were purchased in commercially available single-use aliquots and resuspended in anhydrous DMSO prior to use. AF532, AF680, and BS(PEG)5, and sulfo-EGS were found to be highly reactive >4 months after being prepared as described.\nBMC Purification from a Heterologous Host\nBMCs were purified in a similar way to previously described methods. Briefly, A single colony of R995 + PduST was used to inoculate 100 mL of 2xYT supplemented with kanamycin and 0.5% 1,2-propanediol and grown overnight at 37°C for ~16 hours. The next day, the culture was pelleted and resuspend in 20 mL of 50 mM Tris-HCl pH 8.0, 200 mM KCl, 5 mM MgCl2, 5 mM β-mercaptoethanol (βME), 0.5 mM EDTA, 0.5 mg/mL lysozyme, 0.5 mM PMSF and 50% B-PER (Thermo) (v/v) and gently rocked at RT for 25 minutes and then placed on ice for 5 minutes. gDNA was then cleared by 2×1s pulses in a sonicator. Insoluble matter was pelleted for 20 minutes at 13000xg, 4°C. BMCs were pelleted at 17500xg, 4°C for 40 minutes. Pelleted BMCs were then resuspended in labeling buffer (20 mM phosphate pH 7.4, 50 mM KCl, 5 mM MgCl2) and pelleted again for 20 minutes. The final BMC pellet was resuspend in 1 mL of labeling buffer and stored at 4°C for less than two weeks. BMCs were quantified via Bradford Assay.\nBMCs containing GFPP1−18 were sourced from the same strain containing EYK054. Growth and purification proceeded as above, except growth media volume was doubled and also contained 0.005% L-arabinose to induce GFPP1−18 expression.\nMicroscopy\nFor in vivo imaging of BMCs, R995 + PduST co-transformed with EYK054 were grown overnight then diluted 1:100 in fresh 2xYT supplemented with 0.5% 1,2-propanediol and 0.005% L-arabinose. Cultures were grown for 5.5 hours at 37°C before being pelleted and resuspended in 1x PBS. Samples were then spotted onto an agarose pad and imaged on a Zeiss Axio Observer Z1.\nFor in vitro imaging of purified, fluorescently tagged BMCs, samples were purified and fluorescently labeled with AF488 as described for 30 minutes on ice. After labeling, BMCs were fixed in 2% paraformaldehyde for 30 minutes on ice then pelleted at 20000xg for 15 minutes at 4°C. Supernatant was removed and the BMC pellet was resuspended in labeling buffer. A single pelleting round removed nearly all unincorporated dye. Prepared BMCs were imaging directly on a non-absorbent 1.5 glass coverslip at 63x magnification with oil by a Ziess LSM 980 Airyscan.\nFor TEM, BMC samples were diluted in labeling buffer to 0.1 mg/mL and 5 μL applied to a formvar coated copper grid, 200 mesh (FCF200-CU, Electron Microscope Sciences). Samples were air dried for 15 minutes and excess was wicked off. Salt was removed by 3×5 μL additions of deionized water before staining with 3 μL of 2% uranyl acetate for 10 seconds. Samples were imaged using a JEOL 100CXII electron microscope.\nLabeling Assays\nFor the 1-dye assay, purified BMCs are diluted to 1.0 mg/mL in labeling buffer and split into a denatured control and native sample. The denatured control has SDS added to 0.5% and is heated at 95°C for 5 minutes. Both samples were equilibrated to room temperature before an aliquot of AF532 was added to 125 μM in both samples. Aliquots were taken from each at the designated timepoints and quenched by adding βME to 5 mM. Aliquots were taken and dialyzed against deionized water to remove excess AF532. Samples were separated via standard SDS-PAGE and resulting bands were analyzed in ImageJ.\nFor the 2-dye assay, purified BMCs are diluted to 1.0 mg/mL as above. AF532 was added to 125 μM and BMCs were labeled for 15 minutes at RT before being quenched as above. Samples were then dialyzed against 20 mM Tris-HCl pH 7.4, 0.5% SDS to remove excess βME before being heated at 95°C for 5 minutes. AF680 was then added to 250 μM and samples were labeled for 90 minutes at RT and quenched as above. Samples were then alkylated with 20 mM 2-chloroacetamide for 1 hour at RT protected from light. AF680 was found not to dialyze well so samples were subject to buffer exchange columns to remove excess AF680 and 2-chloroacetamide. Samples were equilibrated into 2D-PAGE rehydration buffer (8 M urea, 0.25% CHAPS, 0.5% 3–10 NL ampholytes, 20 mM DTT, trace bromophenol blue) for 2D separation.\nFor the biotin pulldown, purified BMCs were diluted to 1.0 mg/mL in labeling buffer and equilibrated to RT. Probe A was added to 125 μM and BMCs were labeled for 5 minutes. Reactions were quenched by addition of βME to 625 μM followed by denaturation by adding SDS to 0.5% and heating. Once cooled to RT, probe B was added to 1 mM and reacted for 30 minutes. Samples were then dialyzed overnight into pulldown buffer (20 mM Tris-HCl pH 8.0, 50 mM NaCl). Dialyzed, labeled samples were incubated with equilibrated NeutrAvidin™ Agarose (Pierce) for 15 minutes on ice. Supernatant was collected and the beads were washed once with pulldown buffer. Bound proteins were eluted by mixing the beads with 1x standard SDS-PAGE sample buffer + 2 mg/mL D-biotin and heating for 1 minute at 98°C.\n2D-Electrophoresis and Peptide Mass Fingerprinting\nThe ZOOM® IPGRunner™ System (Invitrogen) was used for 2D separation. Briefly, samples were diluted into rehydration buffer and approximately 15 μg of protein sample was loaded onto a pH 3–10 NL IPG strip and equilibrated for 6 hours at RT. Samples then underwent isoelectric focusing overnight with stepwise increases in voltage and held for final focusing overnight (16 hours, 2000 V). After focusing, strips were retrieved and incubated in 1x NuPAGE LDS Buffer supplemented with 5 mM DTT for 5 minutes prior to separation via 4–20% PAGE. Gels were fluorescently scanned prior to silver staining/destaining of excised spots with SilverQuest™ Silver Staining Kit (Invitrogen).\nExcised gel spots were washed once in 25 mM NH4HCO3 before being dehydrated in acetonitrile for 15 minutes at RT. Alkylation was skipped since samples had already been alkylated. Dehydrated gel pieces were swelled in 50 μL of 20 ng/μL of Trypsin/LysC (Promega) in 25 mM NH4HCO3 for 30 minutes at 37°C then excess supernatant was removed and digestion was allowed to continue overnight at 37°C. After digestion, peptides were desalted and extracted using C18 tips (Pierce) and eluted in 20 μL of 0.1% TFA, 50% acetonitrile. Samples were analyzed in a Bruker Autoflex Speed MALDI-TOF in DHB/CHCA matrices in linear mode. Peaks were analyzed in MS-Fit using the NCBI non-redundant database for Salmonella species. A mass tolerance of ±2 Da was used and a minimum of 3 peptides was needed for a match.\nDynamic Light Scattering\nLabeling samples were prepared as described in other sections. BMC samples were diluted to 0.1 mg/mL in labeling buffer and equilibrated to room temperature immediately prior to measurement. Light scattering measurements were performed on a Nanobrook Omni Particle Sizer with 90° scatter and analyzed using the CONTIN model. Three biological replicates were measured twice for a total of 6 measurements per condition. Each scan was performed for 90 seconds at RT.\nPduA Purification, Labeling, and BMC Pull Down Assay\nThe plasmid encoding PduA S93C was sub-cloned via Gibson assembly, sequence confirmed, and transformed into T7 Express to create strain DT074. Overnights were used to inoculate 500 mL of 2xYT supplemented with antibiotic to an OD600 and grown at 37°C to an OD600 of 0.6–0.8. PduA was expressed by addition of IPTG to 0.5 mM and incubated at 30°C for 4.5 hours. Cultures were then pelleted, frozen/thawed, and resuspended in Buffer A (50 mM Tris-HCl pH 8.0, 1 M NaCl, 5 mM DTT, and 10 mM imidazole) and lysed via sonication. Insoluble matter was pelleted for 20 minutes at 13000xg, 4°C. The soluble lysate was then equilibrated with 1 mL of equilibrated IMAC resin for 30 minutes at 4°C before being applied to a gravity column. Flowthrough was drained off and the beads were washed with 5 mL of Buffer A, 5 mM of Buffer B (Buffer A + 35 mM imidazole) and PduA S93C was eluted in 5 mL Buffer C (Buffer A + 250 mM imidazole). Eluted protein was dialyzed against 20 mM Tris-HCl pH 7.4, 100 mM NaCl, 10% glycerol. Purified PduA were labeling with AF532 at a ratio of 1:2 AF532:PduA for 2 hours on ice. Purified and labeling proteins were stored at −20°C for later use.\nAfter labeling, PduA* was incubated either with or without 1.0 mg/mL Pdu BMCs for 30 minutes on ice at a concentration of 50 μM in BMC labeling buffer. After incubation, samples were pelleted at 20000xg for 15 minutes. Supernatant was removed and BMC pellets were resuspended in labeling buffer and samples were separated on SDS-PAGE.\nBMC Crosslinking\nBMCs are diluted to 1.0 mg/mL in labeling buffer and equilibrated to room temperature. Regardless of crosslinker used, all were equilibrated to room temperature then added immediately such that the final concentration of DMSO never exceeds 2%. Crosslinking reactions proceeded for 45 minutes at room temperature with gentle rocking. Quenching was performed by adding Tris-HCl pH 8.0 to a final concentration of 5 mM.\nMass Spectrometry\nCross-linked samples were acetone precipitated and resuspended in 5% SDS prior to reduction in 200 mM DTT for 1 hour at 57°C. Samples were then alkylated with 500 mM iodoacetamide for 45 minutes in the dark at RT and quenched by addition of 200 mM DTT. 50 μg of sample were then cleaned and digested in S-Trap™ Micro Spin Columns (ProtoFi) according to manufacturer’s protocols. In brief, samples were acidified with 5 μL of 12% phosphoric acid and proteins precipitated by six times dilution with S-Trap buffer (100 mM TEAB in 90% MeOH, pH = 7.1) and captured on porous micro S-Trap spin column beads. S-Trap columns were washed three times with 150 μL of S-Trap buffer followed by digestion in 20 μL of 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate solution containing 1 μg of trypsin (Promega). Digestions were completed in 1 h at 47°C and peptides were eluted, dried, and resuspended in 0.5% acetic acid. Digested peptides were eluted in 40% acetonitrile, 0.5% acetic acid followed by 80% acetonitrile, 0.5% acetic acid, dried, and resuspended in 0.5% acetic acid and stored at −80°C until analysis.\nFor DSBU and BS(PEG)5 samples, approximately 7 μg of the sample was analyzed. Samples were separated online using an EASY-nLC 1200 (Thermo Fisher Scientific) and with a flow rate of 200 nL/min and eluted in a gradient of 2–80% acetonitrile, 0.5% acetic acid over 220 minutes into an Orbitrap Fusion Eclipse spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific). Full MS spectra were acquired with a resolution of 60,000 (at m/z 200), AGC target of 4×105, max. ion time of 50 ms, and scan range of 400–2000 m/z. MS2 spectra were collected with a cycle time of 3 seconds in the ion trap using rapid scan with a resolution of 30,000, AGC target of 5e4, max. ion time of 70 ms, one microscan, 1.6 m/z isolation window and HCD using stepped 15, 30, and 45% normalized collision energy and CID of 25%. A fragment mass filter for 85.0527 and 111.032 to select crosslinked peptides for MS3. MS3 spectra were collected with IT set to rapid, AGC of 2e4 max. ion time of 70 ms, one microscan, 2.5 m/z isolation window, and HCD of 27%. MS/MS spectra were analyzed using Sequest and XlinkX within Proteome Discoverer 2.53. A mass tolerance of 10 Da was set for MS1 and 0.5 Da for MS2. Xlinks were searched using non-cleavable acquisition strategy, 1.5 signal to noise ratio, FDR of 0.01, and variable Xlink modification on lysine of +138.068 for DSS and BS3 and +302.137 for BS(PEG)5. The following modifications were allowed: fixed: carbamidomethyl on cysteines (+57.021); variable: DSS/BS3 Tris on Lysine (+259.142), DSS/BS3 hydrolyzed on lysine (+156.079), BS(PEG)5 Tris on lysine (+424.218), BS(PEG)5 hydrolyzed on lysine (+320.147), oxidation on methionine (+15.995) and deamidated on asparagine (+0.984). While the cross-linker BS3 was used, it led to the identification of redundant cross-links reported within other datasets. The mass spectrometry raw files acquired at the Proteomics Laboratory at NYU are accessible at https://massive.ucsd.edu under accession MassIVE MSV000088460. A list of all unique cross-links identified can be found in Data S1.\nDSSO treated samples were run on an Orbitrap Eclipse mass spectrometer equipped with a Thermo Ultimate 3500 UPLC and FAIMS unit operating under 3 different CVs (−50, −60, and −75). Chromatography was performed using Mass Spec Grade reagents (H2O and 0.1% formic acid for buffer A and 95% acetonitrile with 5% H2O 0.1% formic acid for buffer B). A 75 cm Thermo Fisher Easy-Spray column was utilized for separation. A 200-minute gradient was utilized for separation at a 0.3 μL/min flow rate (0 min 5%B, 10 min 10%B, 150 min 70%B, 158 min 80%B, 161 min 90%B, 171 min 90%B, 172min 5%B, 200 min 5%B). MS1 was collected in the orbitrap at a resolution of 60,000 scanning from 375–1500 m/z with standard AGC target and MIPS active. An intensity threshold of 2.04e4 was utilized along with charge state inclusion between 2 and 8. Dynamic exclusion was held to 45 min with a mass tolerance of 20 ppm. For stepped collision energy MS2 acquisition, HCD assisted collision energies of 25% and 30% were used. MS2 scans were collected in the orbitrap at a resolution of 30,000. A normalized AGC target of 200% was used with a maximum injection time of 70 ms. For the MS2/MS3 acquisition, a fixed CID collision energy of 25% was used and MS2 scans were collected in the orbitrap at 30,000 resolution at standard AGC target and 70 ms max injection time. MIPS was then used and a targeted mass difference of 31.9721 m/z was used for the DSSO crosslinker. A charge state filter of 2–6 was utilized followed by precursor ion exclusion with a 50 ppm mass width. Two MS3 scans were performed in the ion trap operating under rapid scan mode using a fixed CID energy of 35%. An AGC target of 200% was used with a maximum injection time of 120 ms. For the MS2/MS2/MS3 acquisition, a fixed CID collision energy of 25% was used and MS2 scans were collected in the orbitrap at a resolution of 50,000. MIPS was then used and a targeted mass difference of 31.9721 m/z was used for the DSSO crosslinker. A secondary MS2 scan was collected post-targeted mass identification in the orbitrap at a resolution of 30,000 utilizing ETD fragmentation. A defined mass range of 150–2000 m/z was used with a maximum injection time of 100 ms. For the MS3 scan, charge state filtering was performed post targeted mass identification to include charge states between 2 and 6 followed by precursor ion exclusion with a 50 ppm mass width. Two scans were obtained in the ion trap operating under rapid scan rate with fixed CID collision energy of 35%. An AGC target of 200% was used with a maximum injection time of 120 ms. All data was processed in Thermo Proteome Discoverer 2.5 using the Xlinkx node for cross-link identification. The mass spectrometry proteomics data have been deposited to the ProteomeXchange Consortium via the PRIDE partner repository with the dataset identifier PXD029675. Data reported here all have an Xlinkx score greater than 30. A list of all unique cross-links identified can be found in Data S1.\nHydroxyl Radical Footprinting\nAliquots of 45 μL of 5 μM samples in 20 mM Tris-HCl pH 8.0 buffer were used for each reaction condition in triplicates. Samples were reacted with 15 mM, 7.5 mM, 3.25 mM, and 0 mM of iron(II) sulfate heptahydrate and hydrogen peroxide at 2 fold excess, respectively, for 2 minutes. The reaction was quenched with 50 μL of 0.1 ng/μL catalase, 68 μM thiourea, and 34 μM methioninamide. The samples were subsequently incubated with 50 μM Poros Beads for 2 hours at 4°C. The beads were then loaded onto C4 ZipTips, washed 3 times with 0.1% TFA, 3 times with 0.5% acetic acid and eluted with 40% acetonitrile followed by 80% acetonitrile in 0.5% acetic acid and subsequently concentrated in a SpeedVac concentrator. Samples were resuspended in 50 μL 100 mM ammonium bicarbonate, reduced with 20 mM dithiothreitol for 1 hour at 57°C, alkylated with 50 mM iodoacetamide for 45 min at room temperature in the dark, and digested with 1 μg of trypsin (Promega) overnight at room temperature. Next, the peptides were desalted with C18 ZipTips and resuspended in 0.5% acetic acid.\nAn aliquot of the peptide mixture was loaded onto an Evosep tip and separated on an EV-1106 Evosep column using the 15SPD Evosep method. MS1 scans were acquired at 60,000 resolution (@ 400 m/z), 50 msec maximum injection time, AGC target of 4e5, and scan range of 400 to 2000 m/z. MS2 scans were acquired at 30,000 resolution (at 400 m/z), 200 msec maximum injection time, AGC target of 5e4, isolation window of 2 m/z, one microscan, and HCD using 30% normalized collision energy. Raw files were searched using Byonic (Protein Metrics) using the following settings: The MS2 spectra data was searched with a 10 ppm mass tolerance for MS1 and MS2, allowing for 2 missed cleavages. We allowed the following modifications: carbamidomethyl +57.021 on C, −17.0265 on Q, −18.010565 on E, Oxidation +15.9949 on A, E, I, K, L, Q, R, V, C, D, F, H, M, P, W, Y, Dioxidation +31.9898 on C, M, P, W, and Y, Trioxidation +47.9847 on W, Y, P, and C, carbonylation +13.9793 on L, D, I, V, P, R, K, E, and Q, carbon monoxide loss −27.9949 on D and E, carbon dioxide loss −43.9898 on D and E, decarboxylation −30.0106 on D and E. Along with the documented oxidative histone modifications of −23.0160, −22.0320, and +4.978930 and cysteine modifications of −41.0265, −9.0358 and −25.0316. The area under the curve for each observed peptide and modified isoform was calculated in a semi-automatic fashion using Byologic (ProteinMetrics) and the % oxidation for each peptide calculated.\nQUANTIFICATION AND STATISTICAL ANALYSIS\nAll quantification of fluorescent SDS-PAGE was completed in ImageJ. Data reported are mean ± S.D. as at least triplicate experimental replicates. Data collected in ImageJ were processed in GraphPad Prism 9.\n\n[RESULTS]\nRESULTS\nPurification of Salmonella enterica Pdu BMC\nTo work with purified microcompartments in vitro, we sourced S. enterica Typhimurium LT2 Pdu BMCs (Figure 1a) from a strain of E. coli containing the entire cob/cbi and pdu loci under native control. To investigate whether the Pdu BMC form within E. coli, we co-transformed a second plasmid encoding green fluorescent protein (GFP). This particular gfp (encoding GFPP1−18) also included an N-terminal encapsulation peptide (EP) sequence and a C-terminal proteolytic cleavage tag intended to trigger its degradation unless successfully packaged in a BMC. This approach has been used previously to visualize BMCs in vivo. We likewise observed GFP foci within cells that co-expressed the gfpP1−18 gene (Figure 1b, S1b), confirming in vivo formation of Pdu BMC. Pdu BMCs were then purified from cellular lysates as described previously. An assessment of the resulting material by transmission electron microscopy revealed objects of approximately uniform size and morphology (Figure 1c), which were consistent with the typical appearance of microcompartments. When the purified material was analyzed by SDS-PAGE, it resulted in a banding pattern that matched well with the known sizes and abundances of Pdu BMC proteins (Figure 1d). Together, these data show that our purification strategy results in preparation of intact Pdu BMC.\nShell Proteins are More Accessible to Chemical Probes than Inner Cargo\nGiven that the shell is generally described as a static entity, we assumed it would act as a barrier to relatively large chemicals. To that end, we decided to use fluorophores with a reactive maleimide handle (Alexa Fluor 532 Maleimide, AF532) to selectively conjugate surface accessible thiols. AF532 was added to BMC samples and aliquots were quenched at varying time intervals, with optimization for concentration and the time of incubation. These samples were separated and visualized by SDS-PAGE (Figure 2a, S2a). A sample of BMC that had been denatured prior to incubation with AF532 displayed more bands than native BMC, demonstrating that the native BMCs exhibit inaccessible cysteines, presumably located within the interior of the BMC, which can be exposed by denaturation of the samples. Bands that are likely to correspond to shell proteins PduJ, PduB and PduB’ (a translationally truncated isoform of PduB), labeled quickly at early time points (Figure 2a) and constituted most of the overall fluorescence. Certain individual proteins (BMC-H protein PduA and cargo protein PduL) do not contain cysteine residues and cannot be detected using AF532. For comparison, the major shell proteins PduB and PduB’ have a cysteine on both the concave and convex side, while PduJ only has one on its convex face. These data show proof-in-principle that simple fluorescent chemical probes can be used to qualitatively demarcate solvent-accessible proteins.\nWhile shell proteins exhibited a higher initial share of the total fluorescent labeling, bands that are likely to include interior cargo proteins were also labeled when given enough time (Figure 2a, b). This unexpectedly suggested that, while the probe may preferentially label the exterior, it can also access the inner lumen over time. An example of this effect is seen with the highly abundant cargo protein PduC.\nWe also found that shell proteins and cargo proteins label at noticeably different rates, where shell proteins labeled quickly and eventually saturate. The fluorescence of each band was quantified as a fraction of the total fluorescence for each time point (Figure 2b, S2b, S2c). This showed that shell proteins lose their share of total fluorescence as they saturate while cargo components continue to label; therefore, the labeling patterns of interior and exterior differ from one another. Denatured samples showed no such bias towards labeling and intact samples that are denatured can fully recapitulate the denatured state, indicating that AF532 is not limiting. We conclude that the native morphology of the BMC limits the dyes’ access to certain - most likely interior - proteins.\nShell and Cargo Components Can be Chemically Differentiated\nOur initial results hinted that information about the shell and lumen can be independently captured given the right conditions. Accordingly, we adjusted our labeling strategy to include a second probe. In this new scheme, the first probe reacts with and blocks accessible residues after which the total sample is denatured and the remaining cysteines are reacted with a second fluorescent probe (Figure 2c). We reasoned that this strategy should label exterior-facing proteins with the first probe (AF532) and interior proteins with the second probe (Alexa Fluor 680 Maleimide; AF680), which could then be analyzed and quantified by SDS-PAGE (Figure 2d).. The identity of the probes was interchangeable; we found that AF680 acts similarly to AF532 (Figure S2d). Individual proteins are not observable by one-dimensional SDS-PAGE; therefore, we utilized two-dimensional (2D) PAGE to separate individual protein spots. These proteins were resolved first by isoelectric focusing followed by separation by mass (Figure 2d). The overall spot pattern agreed well with previously published data, albeit with an overall shift to more acidic pI’s, likely due to the conjugated dyes. Regardless, the 2D analysis showed that some proteins were preferentially labeled with the surface accessible first probe and other proteins were preferentially labeled by the second probe. For simplicity, we elected to independently quantify the two labels for specific zones of the gel since shell and cargo components tended to segregate into specific areas. For each zone, we quantified a ratio of red/green signal, which we interpreted as enrichment for either shell (green biased) or cargo (red biased) (Figure 2e). This method found that areas rich in shell proteins (zones 3 and 5) were enriched for green fluorescence relative to areas rich in cargo components (zones 1, 2, and 4). Some spots were excised and subjected to peptide mass fingerprinting, confirming the presence of anticipated proteins within specific zones (Figure S2d, S2e). Together, these data show that exterior and interior proteins can be visually resolved by 2D PAGE.\nThis dual-label workflow is not restricted to fluorescent chemical handles.(Figure 2f). To test this hypothesis, we reacted native BMC proteins with maleimide-linked biotin and, after denaturing the entire sample, then incubated with plain maleimide. Biotin-bound proteins were then separated using streptavidin-bound agarose beads. Using this order of chemical probes, the agarose-associated proteins appeared to be enriched for shell proteins (Figure 2f, lane A). However, when the order of the chemical probes was reversed, for selective binding of cargo proteins, the enrichment pattern did not appear to be as strong (Figure 2f, lane B). We speculate that the small size of the plain maleimide probe may allow it to diffuse and block thiols faster when used as the first probe. Therefore, this reaction scheme would need to be optimized further to isolate cargo proteins. Regardless, these data show proof-in-principle that inside and outside information can be independently captured using biotin probes.\nFluorescent Labeling Allows for Direct Confocal Imaging of Native BMCs\nBMCs are large but still beneath the diffraction limit of light microscopy. Due to this, most direct imaging has relied on TEM to study their morphology (e.g., Figure 1). We speculated that the fluorescently labeled native Pdu BMCs may be directly observable using optical interrogation methods in vitro, akin to single-particle analyses. To test this, we labeled Pdu BMC with or without Alexa Fluor 488 maleimide (AF488) and analyzed them using laser scanning confocal microscopy (Figure 2g). Unsurprisingly, unlabeled BMCs exhibited no background fluorescence. The same holds true for BMCs incubated with AF488 that had been pre-reduced by dithiothreitol (DTT), demonstrating that AF488 does not non-specifically associate. In contrast, BMCs labeled with reactive AF488 could be visualized as small, defined foci under the conditions we tested. The same results were found with AF680. This demonstrates that fluorescent chemical probes can be employed for direct visualization of bacterial BMCs using light microscopy.\nThe Protein Shell Displays Chemical Permeability\nThe current line of thought is that BMCs are a closed system that carefully controls the influx and efflux of metabolites. While fluorescent chemical probes exhibited a bias towards reactivity against surface proteins, we were surprised that they appeared to also access the inner lumen. One possibility is that the overall shell structure may be dynamic, allowing the probe to access the interior. To further test this hypothesis, we employed chemical cross-linkers, which we reasoned would reduce dynamics of shell proteins, resulting in an altered fluorescent labeling profile when tagged with AF532. We chose the cross-linker ethylene glycol bis(sulfosuccinimidyl succinate) (sulfo-EGS) since it can be cleaved after the labeling reaction. When sulfo-EGS was varied between 1 to 5 mM, higher concentrations resulted in formation of a band that would not enter the resolving gel (Figure 3a, S3a), indicating successful cross-linking. Cross-linked BMC samples were then incubated with AF532 and aliquots were removed at time intervals. The cross-link was then reversed with hydroxlamine and the resulting mixture was resolved by SDS-PAGE. Band fluorescence was quantified as a ratio of PduB (a major BMC-T shell protein) to either PduC or PduP (major cargo proteins) intensity. We reasoned that this ratio would change if fluorescence reactivity of cargo proteins was reduced by crosslinking at the surface. Consistent with this hypothesis, the labeling ratio was biased towards an increased labeling of PduB relative to either PduC or PduP (Figure 3b and 3c, respectively). From this we speculate that transient cross-linking of the native BMC can reduce permeability of the shell layer.\nThese data suggest that BMCs may exhibit some permeability. It is possible that this permeability results from general heterogeneity (i.e., cracks) within the shell or from damage incurred during the purification process. However, an alternate hypothesis is that the protein shell itself is intentionally dynamic. This latter hypothesis has been indirectly explored in a prior investigation of purified BMC-H proteins by atomic force microscopy (AFM), which showed that purified BMC-H proteins assemble and disassemble in real-time. To test this, we purified an S93C mutant of the BMC-H protein PduA (Figure S3b) and labeled it with AF532 to act as a reporter (PduA*). The interaction between PduA* and native BMCs was tested by incubating PduA* with purified BMCs for 30 minutes, followed by low-speed centrifugation of the BMCs and resolution of proteins by SDS-PAGE (Figure 3d). PduA* was not recovered when native BMCs were not added at the incubation step (Figure 3d, lane 3). However, PduA* co-pelleted when incubated with native BMCs. Furthermore, PduA* appeared as a new Coomasie-stained band in the pellet proteins due to its modestly increased mass (from a hexahistidine N-terminal tag and conjugation by AF532).\nTogether, these experiments suggest that permeability of the Pdu BMC is affected by the status of the layer of shell proteins. However, we acknowledge that our current data cannot differentiate between the free exchange of protein subunits within the shell and binding artifacts caused by damaged shell layers. Therefore, we consider this to be an important open question that should be addressed more thoroughly in further experimentation.\nBMCs Can Withstand a Wide-Range of Chemical Modifications\nTo measure the effect of the reagents used in this study on BMC morphology, we turned to dynamic light scattering (DLS). Using this approach, BMC samples could be investigated for changes in size and polydispersity after derivatization (Figure 3e, 3f). We chose to use DLS instead of TEM in order to avoid the morphological distortions that can be introduced during preparation of samples for TEM, as described previously. When measured by DLS, the peak of the distribution of BMCs (142.2 ± 6.3 nm) did not significantly differ from samples that had been reacted with 125 μM AF532 (146.6 ± 6.6 nm). A sample that had been treated with 2% DMSO alone, which was included in the maleimide-fluorophore assays, also closely resembled native BMCs (144.2 ± 2.3 nm). However, BMCs reacted with a commonly used cross-linker bis(sulfosuccinimidyl)suberate (‘BS3’, 5 mM) exhibited a moderate increase in the peak of the BMC distribution (157.7 ± 6.7 nm) and in polydispersity as well. Since this cross-linking reagent reacts with lysine, this apparent increase in mass may result from the greatly increased presence of lysine-conjugated residues. Regardless, we conclude from these data that BMCs can be modified by chemical probes without major disruptions.\nInsights into the Pdu BMC Interactome by Cross-linking Coupled to Mass Spectrometry\nThe results reported herein have established that BMCs are not only permeable to chemical reagents but can also withstand their modifications. Encouraged by this, we wanted to gain a more quantitative insight into the Pdu BMC interactome and turned to cross-linking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) in hopes of getting structural information. While a prior in silico investigation has suggested some protein-protein interactions, there is scant experimental data on the lumen PPI network. Herein, we optimized the concentrations and reaction times of three commonly used amine cross-linkers DSBU, BS(PEG)5, and DSSO (Figure S4) prior to analysis by high-resolution tandem mass spectrometry. In total, this resulted in discovery of interactions between 16 different proteins that are described by 230 unique cross-links. We sub-divide these cross-links into 161 cargo-cargo links, 13 cargo-shell links, and 56 shell-shell links (Figure 4a). A full description of the non-redundant cross-links summarized in Figure 4a can be found in Data S1.\nEP-Tagged Proteins Favor Cargo-Cargo Interactions\nIt is critical to understand how the proteins with EPs act in a native context. The current paradigm dictates that EPs can be fused to cargo proteins to guide them to a cognate shell protein partner. Within the Pdu BMC, four proteins (PduDELP) encode for N-terminal EPs. Our analysis revealed that a majority of the cross-links specifically occurred between PduCDELP proteins, with few cross-links for the other cargo proteins (Figure 4b). Note, PduC does not encode for an EP and is likely included in these links due to forming a dimer of trimers with PduDE. A notable exception is the reactivation factor PduG, which is known to interact with the signature complex PduCDE by displacing PduD. Consistent with this, we observed cross-links of PduG to either PduC or PduE, but not PduD (Figure 4b). Another exception is the propionate kinase PduW, which has been proposed to be located outside of the inner lumen. However, we observed cross-links between PduW and the PduCDE complex (Figure S5). Interestingly, propanol dehydrogenase PduQ cross-linked to the opposite side of the PduCDE complex, relative to PduW.\nAt first glance, it appears that EPs may act as a general aggregation factor to increase the local concentrations of these proteins to one another and increase their likelihood of cross-linking, thus explaining their enrichment for cross-links. To test this, we co-expressed Pdu BMCs with GFPP1−18 (GFP containing the PduP EP, Figure 1d, Figure 4c) to see if it will integrate into this subnetwork. BMCs purified under these conditions do include GFPP1−18 and, perhaps consequently, a reduced quantity of PduP (red arrow, Figure 4c). XL-MS confirmed that GFPP1−18 incorporated into the EP subnetwork and was captured in cross-links to both PduP and PduL, including cross-links directly between EPs (asterisks, Figure 4b). Note, the N-terminus of GFPP1−18 and PduP cannot be differentiated but we contend that they act in a similar manner. Further, perturbation of the luminal cargo does not influence the cross-links captured among the protein shell as cross-links between PduB138-PduA55 and PduA55-PduJ54 are maintained throughout all datasets. From this, we speculate that the shell may scaffold on, but assemble independently of, the inner core.\nThe 161 cargo-cargo cross-links can be sub-divided into those directly between EPs-fused cargo (cargoEP-cargoEP), between an EP containing cargo protein and another cargo protein (cargoEP-cargo), or between two non-EP containing cargo proteins (cargo-cargo) (Figure 4d). From these cross-links, we looked at those that directly included the EPs of PduDELP and took an inventory of whether they cross-linked to another EP, a non-EP portion of a cargo protein, or a shell protein (Figure 4e). We find that EPs form cross-links to other EPs but also mainly to other EP-containing cargo proteins. Only three cross-links between the shell (PduB, specifically) and EPs were found and are distributed evenly among PduDEL. From this, we speculate that EPs mainly drive cargo-cargo interactions as opposed to cargo-shell interactions.\nThe Convex Face of the Shell Protein PduA Interacts with Inner Cargo\nUnderstanding the orientation of the proteins that comprise the outer shell and their interactions with the enzymatic core will be crucial for any future efforts at redesigning BMC. We found 13 cross-links between shell and cargo components. A majority of these cross-links involved the convex face of the shell proteins (Figure 5a). These links were captured between the cargo proteins PduDELM (Figure 5b) and the major shell components PduABJ (Figure 5c). While PduAJ formed links to only PduD170 (red circles, Figures 5b, c), PduB was captured in cross-links to PduDELM, including links between the EPs of PduDEL directly to the convex surface of PduB263 (Figure 5c). This revealed that the convex face of the protein shell faces inward, while the concave face points towards the cytosol. Further, it may suggest that PduB acts as a potential interaction hub between the shell and cargo. However, we are not able to confirm from these data alone whether EPs are specifically responsible for cargo-shell interactions.\nThe Terminal Extensions of Shell Proteins Follow Sequence-Dependent Rules\nA total of 56 cross-links were identified among the protein shell (Figure 6a). These cross-links are sub-divided based on which face of the shell the cross-links were located. The links designated as “other” corresponded to the N- or C-terminal extensions of PduB and PduK, respectively, which have not yet been structurally resolved. It has been suggested that the extension on PduB interacts with cargo and therefore lies luminally, but the location of PduK’s extension has been unknown. Herein, our data revealed cross-links between the N-terminus of PduB and the convex faces of PduAJ (Figure 6b). Moreover, we observed a cross-link between the C-terminus of PduK with the concave faces of PduAJT (Figure 6b). Together, these data confirm that the PduB extension faces inward, where it can in theory interact with cargo, and suggest that the PduK extension faces outward.\nGiven that our data already established the orientation of the protein shell based on the positions of cargo cross-links, we sought to apply hydroxyl-radical footprinting to further confirm the shell orientation and the placement of the terminal extensions on PduBK (Figure 6c). In this experiment, we measured the oxidation of surface-exposed amino acids using hydroxyl radical generation via the Fenton reaction and determined the percent oxidation using mass spectrometry. Peptides that had the most change (>10%) were deemed exposed. Using PduJ as a case study (due to excellent sequence coverage), our footprinting results showed that the concave-presenting residues on PduJ (M1, L79, P79, and H80; black residues, Figure 6c) exhibited the most oxidation change and therefore are oriented outwardly. In contrast, our data provide additional support that the N-terminal extension of PduB is likely to face toward the BMC lumen.\nPduA also has a short C-terminal tail that has been suggested to interact with cargo EPs. We identified unanticipated cross-links between residues in this region (PduA86 and PduA90) to PduJ (Figure 6d). When high-resolution structures of PduA and PduJ are manually placed adjacent to one another, these residues are further apart than the cross-linker would allow (Figure 6d). We also observed cross-links between PduA90, which is on the concave face, to PduA12, which is on the convex face. From these observations we speculate that the short C-terminus of PduA (and perhaps PduJ) might be disordered or flexible. Support for this also comes from recent molecular dynamics analyses of the C-terminal tail of CcmK2, a homolog of PduA, which showed that it exists in a disordered, flexible state. Together, these data suggest BMC-H termini exist predominantly on the outside of the compartment and may play a role in shell-shell interactions. However, it is not yet clear from our data how this C-terminal extension affects cargo EPs. Further investigations of shell protein extensions and their associated linker sequences are warranted.\nPduA and PduJ interact with Discrete Edges of the Trimeric Shell Protein PduB\nThe precise organization of the Pdu BMC protein shell has continued to elude researchers for decades. Key progress has been achieved through analyses of synthetic derivatives of BMCs, such as the Hoch BMC, for which empty shells were analyzed by high-resolution structural approaches. Therefore, we analyzed our cross-links for insights into shell protein patterns. We captured cross-links suggesting that PduB accommodates two sets of interactions. As a trimer, the way PduB domains assemble into a pseudo-hexameric structure creates two distinct edges. When assessing the cross-links between the shell proteins PduA and PduJ to PduB, it became apparent that certain cross-links can only be explained by PduA and PduJ interacting with specific edges (deemed edge A and B) of PduB (Figure 6e).\nPduB146 and PduB252 occupy the same positions on the two different BMC domain repeats, yet PduA could only cross-link to PduB146 on the first domain (edge B) and PduJ was only found to cross-link to PduB252 in the second domain (edge A, Figure 6e). Further, PduJ can cross-link to the N-terminus of PduB, which is presumably along the same edge as PduB252. Also, PduA0 (i.e., the free amine at the N-terminus) formed a cross-link to PduB177 on edge B. Both PduA and PduJ share a cross-link to PduB138, which at a vertex in the hexagonal structure of PduB, is accessible from both edges. From our aggregate data, we speculate a model where PduAJ occupy alternating positions around the PduB trimer (Figure 6e). Given the stoichiometries of PduABJ, it is easy to imagine this cyclical arrangement as the fundamental unit of the BMC that makes up the bulk of each facet along the polyhedron (Figure 7a).\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
